{
    "title": "Truth in Lending (Regulation Z)",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 15, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-15/pdf/2025-22814.pdf",
    "summary": "Starting January 1, 2026, the Federal Reserve and the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau will raise the dollar cut-off that determines when very large consumer loans and leases are exempt from certain Truth in Lending and Consumer Leasing rules to $73,400, to keep pace with inflation. In a separate move, the Bureau set the maximum price credit reporting companies can charge for an extra copy of your credit file (beyond the free ones you\u2019re entitled to) at $16 for 2026.\n\nPractically, this means most everyday loans and leases remain covered by key consumer protections. Only very large transactions over the new threshold may be exempt, and important exceptions still apply: mortgages, home-secured credit, and private student loans remain covered no matter how big they are. The agencies also reaffirmed that some leases tied to services\u2014like equipment for home TV service, home security systems, or propane tanks\u2014aren\u2019t treated as consumer leases under these rules, and safe deposit boxes aren\u2019t covered as leases either.\n\nConsumers benefit from a threshold that adjusts with living costs, which helps preserve the reach of disclosure and fee rules in real terms. A small slice of bigger-ticket loans and leases that fall just under the new number will continue to receive protections, and the $16 cap offers price certainty if you request additional credit file disclosures beyond your free options.\n\nLenders and lessors gain clarity and predictability from a standardized, annually updated threshold and detailed examples of how the rules apply to open-end and closed-end credit. At the same time, some creditors may see slightly higher compliance demands for transactions that now sit below the new threshold and therefore require disclosures, statements, and limits on certain fees and rate changes. Credit reporting companies are limited to the $16 cap for extra file disclosures, while consumers who need more than their free reports will still pay up to that amount.\n\nThe agencies describe these updates as technical, inflation-driven changes rather than new policy, and they take effect on January 1, 2026.",
